hi this is presh talwalkar  let's play the mathematical game of spin  until you lose  you have a wheel with three values of  end one dollar and two dollars  you spin the wheel until it lands on one  of the values  if it lands on one or two dollars you  bank that amount of money  in this case one dollar and then you  spin the wheel again  if you end up on two dollars on your  next spin  you get to add that amount to your bank  so now we have a total of three dollars  in the bank and we keep spinning the  wheel  if we land on the value of end  that means the game is over and we cash  out whatever value we banked  so in this game we've won three dollars  the puzzle for you  is if you play this game  how much do you expect to win on average  some of the games you're going to end up  you know finishing on end right away  other games you may continue to keep  spinning and win more and more  the puzzle to you is how much money do  you win on average  if you solve that problem also give the  problem of the general case a try  imagine a wheel  that has values that go up from one  dollar all the way up to n dollars  each space on the wheel is equally  likely to be landed on when you spin the  wheel  the same rules apply  you keep spinning the wheel and banking  money until you land on the value of end  in which case you cash out the banked  amount  how much money do you win in the general  case  give these problems a try and when  you're ready keep watching the video for  the solution  if you try and solve this by explicitly  calculating how many ways you can win  one dollar two dollars and so on it's  going to be an infinite series that's  very difficult to solve  so there's a  trick let's imagine we have a variable x  which denotes the average winnings of  our gameplay  we can split up x into the three  different outcomes for the first turn  this is known as the law of total  expectation  so we can either consider x as landing  on end on the first term we can consider  landing on one dollar on the first term  or we can consider landing on two  dollars on the first term  each of these are equally likely  now if we land on end then on the first  turn there's a one-third chance that  we're going to end up with zero dollars  we simply end the game  what happens if we land on one dollar on  our first turn  in that case  we're going to get one dollar in our  bank and essentially we start the game  exactly over because once you banked one  dollars  you it's like you started the game from  scratch you're going to expect going  forward a total value of x dollars  so if you land on one on your first turn  then you're going to expect one dollar  more than x  so there's a one-third chance you'll end  up  getting x plus one dollars  similarly if you land on two dollars on  your first turn  it's as if the game has started over  except you have an extra two dollars  so your expectation in this case will be  that you're going to expect to make two  dollars more than x  so we combine this in the expectation we  have an expression for x  we can now simplify this  i'm going to group out the one-third  term i'm going to have 2x and then we  have the sum of the dollar values of 0 1  and 2.  we end up solving that x is equal to 3  and this is interesting our average  value is equal to the sum of one and two  dollars which are the values on the  wheel  is this true for the general case let's  find out and prove that it actually is  true for the general case  so if we have a wheel that goes from one  dollar all the way up by one dollar  increments to n dollars  we denote x as our average winnings  and then we're going to split up x into  all the different values that we land on  the first turn  these are all equally likely  and it's going to give us an expectation  based on what we land on for the first  turn  if we land on n dollars remember we're  going to expect n dollars more than x so  we now have this interesting expression  and the numbers on the right go from 0 1  all the way up to n  so these are the number this is equal to  the sum of the numbers from 1 to n which  is a familiar formula of n times n plus  one over two  we simplify this expression and we find  out  that the amount that you expect to win  is equal to n times n plus one over two  which is the sum of all the values on  the wheel  so it's a kind of interesting result  that this probability game you end up  with the sum of the numbers from 1 to n  did you figure it out  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books i've  provided links in the video description
 

